# Fix-or-drop policy: a check whose findings are all intentional gets
# disabled here with a reason, never nolint'd in the code.
#
# Disabled and why:
# - readability-magic-numbers: TLS wire constants, rotation counts, and
#   field-arithmetic radices are the domain; naming each would hurt more.
# - readability-identifier-length: a, b, r, h, t, i are idiomatic in
#   crypto field arithmetic and byte loops.
# - readability-implicit-bool-conversion: plain-C truthiness is house style.
# - bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters: (key, nonce), (in, out) pairs are
#   the natural crypto API; tests and vectors catch swaps immediately.
# - clang-analyzer-security.insecureAPI.DeprecatedOrUnsafeBufferHandling:
#   recommends Annex K _s functions that glibc, macOS, and newlib never
#   shipped; bounds live in the rbuf/wbuf layers instead.
# - bugprone-suspicious-include: test/unit_test.c and proof harnesses include
#   the .c files under test on purpose, to reach static internals.
# - misc-include-cleaner: reasons about textual includes, not POSIX
#   contracts; false-positives on umbrella headers.
# - bugprone-not-null-terminated-result: assumes a memcpy from a string
#   should produce a C string. hkdf assembles length-prefixed binary wire
#   fields from label text; a terminator byte there would corrupt the
#   encoding. Fires on Linux builds of clang-tidy 22 and not on macOS
#   builds of the same version, so this is the check's judgment, not a
#   version gap.
# - bugprone-signed-bitwise: 351 findings across the tree, none a defect. The
#   crypto cores hold 245 of them and mean them: x25519 carries `int64_t
#   fe[16]` limbs, and ct.h's widening multiply builds its corrections from
#   two's-complement masks on signed values, which is what keeps a limb's sign
#   off the control path. Most of the rest are integer promotion -- a uint8_t
#   or uint16_t widens to signed int, and the value cannot be negative.
#   The check's one genuinely undefined case, left-shifting a negative value,
#   was already found and fixed by hand at x25519.c:23-26, with the reasoning
#   written beside it.
#   What replaces the check is stronger than the check. Every module carrying
#   a finding runs a CBMC harness under run.sh's `full` set, which proves
#   absence of signed overflow and UB over unconstrained inputs at the
#   module's real bound: aead, buf, chacha20, ct, handshake_parser,
#   handshake_post, hkdf, io, mlkem, mlkem_poly, p256, poly1305, record, rsa,
#   sha256, sha3, x509, and x25519's ops and mul lemma. rsa_mont.c and
#   handshake_message.c are covered too, through the rsa_mul and hello_build
#   harnesses that compile them. One shipped source has no harness, tls.c, so
#   its two sites were read by hand: both are `server_pubkey[len - 1] & 1` on
#   a uint8_t array element, checking an RSA modulus is odd.
#   `make lint-proof-cover` holds that claim: it fails when a shipped source is
#   neither compiled by a `full` harness nor recorded in tools/proof-cover.py's
#   audited list.
# - readability-trailing-comma: 138 findings, every one cosmetic, and its
#   automatic fix is unsafe on this tree. Most of them sit in test vector
#   headers, which do not parse standalone -- they read identifiers the
#   including .c file declares -- so clang-tidy builds a broken AST and applies
#   its fix at the wrong offset. Run against test/x509_chain_tests.h it turned
#   `size_t n = put_entry(...)` into `sizeof n = put_entry(...)`. Applying 138
#   style edits by hand across the vector tables buys nothing a reader values,
#   and clang-format, which is this tree's formatting authority, has no opinion
#   on the trailing comma either way.
# - clang-analyzer-optin.taint.GenericTaint: new in clang-tidy 23, and it can
#   only ever fire outside the library. chapulin makes no system call at all --
#   the caller supplies every I/O callback -- so no shipped source reaches
#   execl, system or popen. Its one finding is test/diff_driver.h:124, where
#   the differential driver execs the Lean spec binary named by argv[1]
#   (test/diff_test.c:36, defaulting to spec/.lake/build/bin/diffspec). That is
#   the developer naming a path on their own command line.
# - readability-math-missing-parentheses: byte-packing indices like
#   p[4 * i + 1] saturate this codebase; the precedence is grade-school and
#   the parens would be pure noise.
# softmul.c is excluded from diagnostics rather than any check being
# disabled. It must define __mulsi3 and __muldi3 -- the names the compiler
# emits, so they replace the runtime library's on a core with no hardware
# multiplier -- and those are reserved identifiers with external linkage by
# necessity. bugprone-reserved-identifier and misc-use-internal-linkage stay
# on for every other file; the test includes the unit, so the exclusion has
# to be by path rather than by target.
ExcludeHeaderFilterRegex: 'softmul\.c'
Checks: >
  -*,
  bugprone-*,
  cert-*,
  clang-analyzer-*,
  misc-*,
  performance-*,
  portability-*,
  readability-*,
  -bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters,
  -bugprone-not-null-terminated-result,
  -bugprone-signed-bitwise,
  -bugprone-suspicious-include,
  -clang-analyzer-security.insecureAPI.DeprecatedOrUnsafeBufferHandling,
  -clang-analyzer-optin.taint.GenericTaint,
  -misc-include-cleaner,
  -readability-identifier-length,
  -readability-implicit-bool-conversion,
  -readability-magic-numbers,
  -readability-math-missing-parentheses,
  -readability-trailing-comma
WarningsAsErrors: '*'
CheckOptions:
  readability-function-cognitive-complexity.Threshold: '15'
  # Without this, the if inside every expanded CHECK() dominates the test
  # functions' scores; production macros here contain no control flow.
  readability-function-cognitive-complexity.IgnoreMacros: 'true'
